A case study is an opportunity to put your Power BI skills into action.

4
00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:16.840
You’ll apply the skills you learned in previous courses and practice them on a real-world problem.

5
00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:22.280
Here are some of the prerequisite courses that we recommend before completing this case study.

6
00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:26.760
In this case study, a fictitious consulting company called HealthStat has

7
00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:32.840
hired you to uncover insights on potential hospital efficiency opportunities.

8
00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:37.160
By the end of this course, you will successfully analyze a state-wide hospital

9
00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:44.080
dataset and leverage your Power BI skills to create an engaging dashboard of insights.

10
00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:51.200
In healthcare analytics, quality metrics are often classified across these six domains.

11
00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:57.040
For this case study, we'll be focusing on efficiency, which is all about avoiding waste.

12
00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:02.760
This includes minimizing waste of equipment, supplies, ideas and energy.

13
00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:09.760
Length of stay (or LOS) is considered an important indicator of the efficiency of hospital management.

14
00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:14.840
It's calculated as the total duration in days for a patient stay in hospital.

15
00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:18.880
A shorter LOS is often desirable in hospital operations.

16
00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:21.760
Primarily, shorter LOS means costs can be lowered.

17
00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:30.160
Reducing LOS can also release capacity in the system and improve throughput, enabling hospitals to serve more patients.

18
00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:32.640
Many factors can impact LOS.

19
00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:37.800
Patient age, health status, the type of procedure (or surgery), whether or not

20
00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:43.320
there were any complications, and the size of the hospital are some common factors.

21
00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:45.880
Here are some terms you will encounter throughout this case

22
00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:46.200
study:

23
00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:47.080


24
00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:51.640
An inpatient is a person who has been admitted to a hospital bed.

25
00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:56.920
A discharge, is defined as the release of a patient from hospital care by a medical worker.

26
00:01:56.920 --> 00:02:04.560
Disposition, is the patient destination or status upon discharge, for instance to another facility or to home.

27
00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:12.280
An elective surgery is a procedure that was planned in advance, in other words, it was not due to an emergency.

28
00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:18.320
In this case study, the focus is on patients who received hip replacement surgery.

29
00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:21.320
Here's a bit of a background on what this procedure is all about.

30
00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:29.120
Patients with hip pain, typically arthritis, may require elective hip replacement surgery.

31
00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:36.760
In this procedure, damaged bone and cartilage is surgically removed and replaced with prosthetic components.

32
00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:42.280
Hospital stay can range from 0 to 2 or more days.

33
00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:45.720
For this case study, we will be working with a dataset that

34
00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:51.000
includes New York state-wide hospital discharge data for a year.

35
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:55.440
Elective hip replacement surgery was the main reason for their hospital stay.

36
00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:59.000
The dataset is one single table with 30 columns

37
00:02:59.000 --> 00:02:59.640


38
00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:01.320
Each row in the dataset

39
00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:07.160
represents a single inpatient stay, from their admission to discharge date.

40
00:03:07.160 --> 00:03:11.800
The health information in this dataset is not individually identifiable.

41
00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:16.400
This means the file does not contain personal health information.

42
00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:20.760
The dataset has some key attributes to analyze efficiency.

43
00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:23.440
Length of stay measured in total days

44
00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:28.880
Total costs attributed to each hospital stay.

45
00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:33.320
Listed here are some of the key attributes of interest for the case study.

46
00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:38.480
These include a unique identifier for facility, a grouping of patient age,

47
00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:45.480
patient's disposition, the diagnosis description, severity of illness, and risk of mortality.

48
00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:49.200
We will closely evaluate these (and others) in the case study as we

49
00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:54.920
work to understand what factors impact length of stay and related costs.

50
00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:59.280
Your final deliverable for this case study will be to create a dashboard that enables

51
00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:00.240
your stakeholders to:

52
00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:00.240


53
00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:04.560
Progress through key insights in a logical flow,

54
00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:04.960
View

55
00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:07.160
informative, concise visuals

56
00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:12.960
Get data they need to make business decisions for improving efficiency.

57
00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:17.080
It's time to check your understanding of terms introduced so far.

58
00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:19.840
Let's start analyzing!

